Specification Transactions

Description The transaction and the rule that every transaction balances.
# Splits and transfers are not separate kinds of thing here: a split
# is a transaction whose Destination is Split, with its lines in
# TransactionSplit; a transfer is a pair of transactions each naming
# the other as Destination.
Details
# Tables in this file group rows into one transaction with a Ref column.
# Ref is how a table says that two rows belong together, and nothing more
# than that: a device of these tables, short enough to read. It is not
# shown anywhere in the program and it is not what a transaction is stored
# under. That is its Id, given once when the transaction is created and
# never changed afterwards. See the rule on naming a transaction.

DataType TransactionRef
Description How a table in this specification says which rows belong to one
# transaction. Not the stored identity, which is the Id.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value | IsValid | Notes                               |
| T1    | true    | Short names are used in these tables |
| 4091  | true    | The program assigns numbers         |
|       | false   | A row always belongs to one         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shape of a transaction
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DataType ImportSource
Description Where an identifier came from. Two files describing one transaction
# do not agree on what to call it, so the name of the source travels
# with the name of the transaction.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value | Notes                                      |
| Ofx   | A FITID, from an OFX or QFX download        |
| Csv   | Whatever the bank's CSV calls a reference   |
| Qif   | From a QIF migration, where one is present |

Entity ImportId
Description An identifier a file gave to a transaction, and which file gave it.
Details At most one per source: a transaction has exactly one home account --
# the one it was downloaded into or typed against -- so there is no
# second side with an identifier of its own.
| Attribute | Type         | Default | Notes                                |
| Source    | ImportSource | Ofx     | Which kind of file said so           |
| Id        | Text         |         | As that file gave it, unchanged      |

Collection ImportIds
Description The identifiers one transaction has collected. More than one,
# because a transaction imported from a CSV and later recognised in a
# QFX has a name in each, and losing either would make that file
# import twice.
| DataType | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                        |
| ImportId | 0       | 8       | One per source at most       |

Entity Transaction
Description What a transaction is: the account it lives in, the other side,
# and the signed amount between them.
| Attribute     | Type           | Default    | Notes                        |
| Id            | TransactionId  | none       | Identity; given once, never changes. These tables never name one: it is given by whatever creates the transaction, so a table that stated one would be stating something no scenario controls |
| Ref           | TransactionRef |            | A device of these tables; not stored |
| Date          | Date           | 2024-01-01 | The date it is posted on     |
| Payee         | PayeeName      | none       | Optional                     |
| CheckNo       | CheckNumber    | none       | Optional                     |
| Memo          | Text           | none       | For the transaction as a whole |
| Tag           | Text           | none       | A Quicken class, kept on migration; see ImportQif |
| SourceAccount | AccountPath    | none       | The account this entry lives in: downloaded into, or typed in its register. An id underneath, the same as every account reference; see the identity rule in Accounts |
| Destination   | AccountPath    | none       | The other side: a category, a real account, or the literal Split when the parts are in TransactionSplit below |
| Amount        | Money          | 0.00       | Signed against SourceAccount; Destination carries the opposite effect by construction |
| Cleared       | ClearedStatus  | Uncleared  | Per entry: the transaction is one account's own side, and clearing is that account's bank reporting it |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two kinds of unknown
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule An unknown category and an unknown account are different unknowns
Description A charge whose category nobody has chosen is Uncategorized. A payment
# whose other account nobody has worked out is Unassigned. They are
# separate because they are different questions with different answers
# and different ways of being resolved.
Details Uncategorized asks what the money was for. It is answered by choosing a
# category, by a payee rule, or by leaving it: a spending report with a
# line called Uncategorized is telling the truth about money that was
# spent on something nobody classified, and Quicken's own reports have had
# such a line for twenty years.
#
# Unassigned asks which account the money went to. It is answered by the
# other side of the transfer turning up in a later import, or by a person
# assigning it in the register. It is never answered by leaving it, because
# money that left one account and arrived nowhere is not a fact about the
# world -- it is a fact about what has been imported so far.
#
# That difference is why Unassigned is a real account and Uncategorized is
# a category. Unassigned holds money in transit, its balance is the work
# outstanding, and that balance is expected to reach zero. A book whose
# Unassigned balance is not zero is not wrong: it is telling its owner how
# many halves of transfers are still waiting for the other half.
Examples: TwoUnknowns
| TheUnknown     | TheQuestion                      | WhereItLives | ClearedBy                        |
| Uncategorized  | What was the money for           | A category   | A category, a payee rule, or left |
| Unassigned     | Which account did the money go to | A real account | The other import, or the register |

Attributes TwoUnknowns
| Attribute   | Type   | Default |
| TheUnknown  | Text   |         |
| TheQuestion | String |         |
| WhereItLives | Text  |         |
| ClearedBy   | String |         |

Attributes AccountAssignment
Description Assigning the other account of a transfer by hand, in the register.
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| Account   | AccountPath    |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Money from outside the book
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
BusinessRule Whether the other side is an account or a category
Description The other side of a downloaded row is an account when the money came
# from or went to something this book keeps, and a category when it did
# not. That single question decides which of the two unknowns is used,
# and nothing else about the row decides it.
Details A payment from the bank to a credit card the book keeps is a transfer and
# nothing else: both halves are accounts here, the money never left the
# book, and the two halves add to nothing. The book balances because it
# should -- that is the whole of double entry and there is no income or
# expense anywhere in it.
#
# A paycheck is not a transfer. The money came from an employer this book
# does not keep, so there is no second account for it to have come out of,
# and the other side is a category: Income:Salary. The same goes for a
# royalty, and for a distribution from an IRA or a trust that the book does
# not itself hold -- money arriving from outside is income, however
# institutional the sender.
#
# This is why a payee that matches no account's PaymentPayee is not a
# failed transfer. It is an ordinary deposit, and recording it as a
# category is correct rather than second best. Unassigned is only for a row
# that is known to have another side in this book and does not yet know
# which.
Examples: WhereTheOtherSideLives
| TheRow                                | TheOtherSide          | Because                               |
| A payment to a card the book keeps    | The card account      | Both halves are in the book           |
| A transfer between two bank accounts  | The other bank account | Both halves are in the book          |
| A paycheck                            | An income category    | The employer is not in the book       |
| A royalty                             | An income category    | The publisher is not in the book      |
| A distribution from an IRA not held here | An income category | The IRA is not in the book            |
| A distribution from an IRA held here  | The IRA account       | It is in the book, so it is a transfer |
| A card charge                         | An expense category   | The shop is not in the book           |

Attributes WhereTheOtherSideLives
| Attribute    | Type   | Default |
| TheRow       | String |         |
| TheOtherSide | String |         |
| Because      | String |         |

Scenario A deposit from outside the book is a category, not an unassigned transfer
Description A paycheck. Nothing in the book is the other side of it, so there is
# nothing for Unassigned to be waiting for and the row is categorised
# like any other.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                 | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking      | Checking | false       |
| Income:Uncategorized | Income | false       |
| Unassigned           | OtherAsset | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee        | Destination          | Amount  | Cleared |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-04-15 | ACME PAYROLL | Income:Uncategorized | 3000.00 | Cleared |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account    | RawBalance |
| Unassigned | 0.00       |
# Nothing is waiting. Choosing Income:Salary later is a category being chosen,
# not a transfer being completed, and the book balanced all along.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Matching an entry in another register by hand
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
BusinessRule Assigning an account asks whether the other entry already exists
Description Assigning a real account to an Unassigned side says where the money
# went. It does not say whether that account already has a row for it.
# Both are possible and they are different outcomes, so the program
# asks rather than choosing.
Details Making a new entry is right when the other account has no row for this
# money -- usually because it has not been imported yet. The transfer
# is one transaction naming the other account, shown in both registers,
# and the second account will recognise its own row when its file is read.
#
# Matching an existing entry is right when the other account already has
# the row, entered by hand or imported too long ago or too far out for the
# windows to have paired them. Then there are two transactions describing
# one movement of money, and leaving both would double it. They become one.
#
# So the register offers the other account's entries to scroll through,
# nearest in date and amount first, and the user picks the one that is the
# same movement of money. Picking nothing and making a new entry instead is
# always available, because a user who cannot find it is better served by a
# new row than by being made to choose wrongly.
Examples: AssignmentOutcome
| TheOtherAccount                | WhatHappens                                        |
| Has no row for this money      | One transaction names it, and shows in both registers    |
| Has the row, found by the user | Each points at the other's account; neither is deleted |
| Has the row, not found         | A new entry is made; the duplicate is left to be seen later |

Attributes AssignmentOutcome
| Attribute       | Type   | Default |
| TheOtherAccount | String |         |
| WhatHappens     | String |         |

Attributes EntryMatch
Description Matching an Unassigned side against an entry the user picked from
# another account's register.
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| OtherRef  | TransactionRef |         |

Scenario The entries offered to match are the other account's, nearest first
Description Scrolling someone through four hundred rows is not offering a choice.
# The entries offered are the ones in the account being assigned, with
# the closest in amount and date at the top.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       |
| Unassigned             | OtherAsset | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense    | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount          | Date       | Payee            | Destination            | Amount   | Cleared |
| T1  | Assets:Checking        | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Unassigned             | -1200.00 | Cleared |
| T2  | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Unassigned             | 1200.00  | Cleared |
| T3  | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 2026-04-26 | SOME SHOP        | Expenses:Uncategorized | -45.00   | Cleared |
When entries offered for : AccountAssignment Vertical
| Ref     | T1                     |
| Account | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
Then entries offered are : OfferedEntry
| Ref | Date       | Payee            | Amount  |
| T2  | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | 1200.00 |
| T3  | 2026-04-26 | SOME SHOP        | -45.00  |
# Both are in the card, so both can be chosen; the one whose amount is equal and
# opposite to the unassigned side is first, because it is the one meant nine
# times in ten.

Attributes OfferedEntry
| Attribute | Type           | Default    |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |            |
| Date      | Date           | 2024-01-01 |
| Payee     | PayeeName      | none       |
| Amount    | Money          | 0.00       |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Naming a transaction
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
BusinessRule A transaction is named when it is created and keeps that name
Description The name is the moment it was created: the date and the time to the
# millisecond. The generator waits if the clock has not moved since the
# last name it gave out, so no two can be the same, and a name sorts
# into the order things were entered.
Details The name is given once, by whatever creates the transaction, and never
# changes. That is the whole of the rule, and the reason for it is that
# the alternatives cannot promise it. A name taken from where a
# transaction came from -- a FITID, a CSV row -- is a fact about a file,
# and a transaction can collect one of those from every kind of file it is
# ever recognised in. A transaction imported from a CSV and later matched
# in a QFX has a name in each, which is why ImportIds is a collection.
# Identity cannot be one of several, and it cannot change when a later
# import learns something, so it is kept apart from all of them.
#
# The import identifiers are still kept and still do the work they always
# did: they are what makes the same file read twice import nothing the
# second time. They are facts about the transaction rather than its name.
#
# The cost is a millisecond for each transaction created in a run. That is
# nothing while a person types, and about forty-three seconds for the
# forty-three thousand transactions of the business QIF -- paid once, on
# the slowest thing the program already does.
#
# What it buys is worth more than the second. A transaction can be traced:
# a name carrying the minute it was entered answers "when did this appear,
# and what else appeared with it" without anything having had to record it.
Examples: TransactionNaming
| CreatedBy       | TheNameComesFrom              | Notes                              |
| Typing it in    | The clock, to the millisecond | Nothing else is consulted          |
| An OFX import   | The clock, to the millisecond | The FITID is kept, but as a fact   |
| A CSV import    | The clock, to the millisecond | The row id is kept, but as a fact  |
| A QIF migration | The clock, to the millisecond | A millisecond apart, in file order |

Attributes TransactionNaming
| Attribute        | Type   | Default |
| CreatedBy        | String |         |
| TheNameComesFrom | String |         |
| Notes            | String | none    |

Scenario Two transactions created in the same millisecond cannot happen
Description The generator waits for the clock rather than giving out a name it
# has given before. So transactions created as fast as the program can
# create them are still a millisecond apart, and in order.
When transactions are created in a row : TransactionsCreated Vertical
| HowMany | 3 |
Then the names are all different
Then the names increase
# Which is why the database can hold the Id unique and never refuse a write
# that a person did nothing wrong to cause.

Attributes TransactionsCreated
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| HowMany   | Integer | 2       |

DataType TransactionId
Description The name a transaction is stored under: the date and the time it was
# created, to the millisecond. Sorts into creation order as text.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value              | IsValid | Notes                                     |
| 20261005T194233123 | true    | The fifth of October, to the millisecond  |
| 20240102T000000000 | true    | Midnight is a time like any other         |
|                    | false   | A transaction always has one              |
| T1                 | false   | That is a Ref, which is a different thing |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The balancing rule
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule Every transaction balances
Description A transaction names one signed amount against one source account, and
# its destination carries the opposite effect by construction. There is
# no second figure to disagree with the first, so an ordinary
# transaction cannot be out of balance -- it is the shape that makes
# double entry hold, not a check run over a list of transactions.
Details What can still fail to add up is a split, because a split is the one
# place with more than one figure that is supposed to agree with
# another: its lines against its transaction's amount. That check, and
# the refusal when it fails, are the split rule and the shortfall
# scenario further down this file.
#
# The old shape made the same guarantee by summing the amounts of the
# postings of one transaction to zero. Two postings that summed to five
# dollars, or a lone transaction that summed to itself, were the two ways
# that could fail; neither is expressible now. A transaction has one
# amount, and the entry in the other account -- when there is one, as
# for a transfer -- is a transaction in its own right with its own.
Examples: BalanceByConstruction
| Amount   | DestinationEffect | WhatCanDisagree | Notes                                          |
| -45.00   | 45.00             | Nothing         | An ordinary payment                            |
| 1000.00  | -1000.00          | Nothing         | A deposit                                      |
| -1200.00 | 1200.00           | Nothing         | A transfer; the other side is its own transaction |
| 0.00     | 0.00              | Nothing         | A zero transaction balances                    |
| -184.50  | 184.50            | The split lines | Only a split has a second figure to disagree   |

Attributes BalanceByConstruction
| Attribute         | Type   | Default |
| Amount            | Money  | 0.00    |
| DestinationEffect | Money  | 0.00    |
| WhatCanDisagree   | Text   | none    |
| Notes             | String | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Entering a transaction the way the register does
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes RegisterEntry
Description What is typed on one line of a register. The register is always
# opened on one account, which is the Account column here.
Details Payment and Deposit are the two amount columns of the register, and
# only one of them is filled in. See TransactionRegister.
| Attribute | Type        | Default    |
| Account   | AccountPath |            |
| Date      | Date        | 2024-01-01 |
| Payee     | PayeeName   | none       |
| CheckNo   | CheckNumber | none       |
| Category  | AccountPath | none       |
| Payment   | Money       | 0.00       |
| Deposit   | Money       | 0.00       |
| Memo      | Text        | none       |

BusinessRule A register entry becomes one transaction
Description How the Payment and Deposit columns turn into one signed amount.
Details The amount on the register account is the deposit less the payment.
# The register is always opened on one account, so that account is the
# transaction's SourceAccount and the Category column is its
# Destination. The destination carries the opposite effect by
# construction; it is not a second figure for the register to fill in.
Examples: RegisterEntryToTransaction
| Payment | Deposit | Amount  | Notes            |
| 45.00   | 0.00    | -45.00  | Money out        |
| 0.00    | 1000.00 | 1000.00 | Money in         |
| 0.00    | 0.00    | 0.00    | An empty line    |
| 45.00   | 10.00   | -35.00  | Both filled: net |

Attributes RegisterEntryToTransaction
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Payment   | Money  | 0.00    |
| Deposit   | Money  | 0.00    |
| Amount    | Money  | 0.00    |
| Notes     | String | none    |

Scenario A payment entered in a register
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking    |
| Date     | 2024-01-15         |
| Payee    | Harris Teeter      |
| Category | Expenses:Groceries |
| Payment  | 45.00              |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-01-15         |
| Payee         | Harris Teeter      |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking    |
| Destination   | Expenses:Groceries |
| Amount        | -45.00             |
| Cleared       | Uncleared          |

Scenario A deposit entered in a register
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary   | Income | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking |
| Date     | 2024-01-31      |
| Payee    | Acme Payroll    |
| Category | Income:Salary   |
| Deposit  | 3000.00         |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-01-31      |
| Payee         | Acme Payroll    |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Income:Salary   |
| Amount        | 3000.00         |
| Cleared       | Uncleared       |

Scenario A check records its number
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking     | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Rates | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking     |
| Date     | 2024-02-01          |
| Payee    | County Tax Office   |
| CheckNo  | 2041                |
| Category | Expenses:Home:Rates |
| Payment  | 1250.00             |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T1                  |
| Date          | 2024-02-01          |
| Payee         | County Tax Office   |
| CheckNo       | 2041                |
| Memo          | none                |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking     |
| Destination   | Expenses:Home:Rates |
| Amount        | -1250.00            |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transfers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DomainTerm Transfer : Transaction
Description A transaction whose SourceAccount and Destination are both real
# accounts. It is not a separate kind of record: it is recognised by
# its shape, and that is why it needs no special handling anywhere
# else.

Scenario A transfer between two accounts
Description The category column of the register holds the other account. Two
# transactions are created, one for each account, each naming the
# other. A transaction is one account's own entry, so a movement
# between two accounts is an entry in each -- which is also what a
# download of the same movement from both banks would leave, and why
# each side can clear on its own statement.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Checking | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking |
| Date     | 2024-03-01      |
| Category | Assets:Savings  |
| Payment  | 500.00          |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Date       | SourceAccount   | Destination     | Amount  | Cleared   |
| 2024-03-01 | Assets:Checking | Assets:Savings  | -500.00 | Uncleared |
| 2024-03-01 | Assets:Savings  | Assets:Checking | 500.00  | Uncleared |
Then transaction is a transfer : TransferCheck Vertical
| IsTransfer | true           |
| FromAccount | Assets:Checking |
| ToAccount  | Assets:Savings |

Attributes TransferCheck
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| IsTransfer  | Boolean     | false   |
| FromAccount | AccountPath | none    |
| ToAccount   | AccountPath | none    |

Scenario A payment to a category is not a transfer
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking    |
| Date     | 2024-03-01         |
| Category | Expenses:Groceries |
| Payment  | 45.00              |
Then transaction is a transfer : TransferCheck Vertical
| IsTransfer | false |

Scenario A credit card payment is a transfer, not an expense
Description Paying the card moves money between two real accounts. Recording it
# as an expense would count the spending twice: once when each charge
# was categorised, and again when the card was paid.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path             | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking  | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa | CreditCard | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking  |
| Date     | 2024-03-05       |
| Payee    | Visa             |
| Category | Liabilities:Visa |
| Payment  | 300.00           |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Date       | Payee | SourceAccount    | Destination      | Amount  | Cleared   |
| 2024-03-05 | Visa  | Assets:Checking  | Liabilities:Visa | -300.00 | Uncleared |
| 2024-03-05 | Visa  | Liabilities:Visa | Assets:Checking  | 300.00  | Uncleared |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account          | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes                |
| Assets:Checking  | -300.00    | -300.00        | Paid out             |
| Liabilities:Visa | 300.00     | -300.00        | Owed by 300.00 less  |

Scenario A transaction may not name the same account on both sides
Description An account transferring to itself would change nothing and clear
# nothing; it is always a mistake.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
When transaction entered : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T1              |
| Date          | 2024-03-01      |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Assets:Checking |
| Amount        | -500.00         |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                  |
| A transfer must name two different accounts             |

Scenario A transaction to a placeholder account is refused
Description A placeholder exists to group its children in a report. Entering
# against it makes its own balance differ from the total of its
# children, which is the one thing a tree of accounts must not do.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto      | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking |
| Date     | 2024-03-01      |
| Category | Expenses:Auto   |
| Payment  | 45.00           |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                             |
| Expenses:Auto is a placeholder; choose one below it |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splits
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes SplitLine
Description One category line of a split, as it is typed into the split window.
Details The amount is entered the way the register entry is: positive for money
# leaving the account being reconciled.
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Category  | AccountPath |         |
| Amount    | Money       | 0.00    |
| Memo      | Text        | none    |

Scenario A split across three categories
Description One trip to the hardware store, charged three ways. The transaction
# carries the total against the register's account; each category is a
# line of its split.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                       | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking            | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance  | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden       | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs      | Expense | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-02      |
| Payee   | Home Depot      |
| Payment | 184.50          |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category                  | Amount | Memo          |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00 | Paint         |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50  | Compost       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs     | 30.00  | Wiper blades  |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-04-02      |
| Payee         | Home Depot      |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Split           |
| Amount        | -184.50         |
Then split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination               | Amount | Memo         |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00 | Paint        |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50  | Compost      |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs     | 30.00  | Wiper blades |

