$WIP
BusinessRule A split is saved only when its lines match the total it was opened on
Description A split is edited in a window of its own, on a transaction that
# already has an amount, so the window knows what its lines are
# supposed to come to. That total is checked when the window is
# saved, and a split that does not match it is not quietly written.
Details The shortfall is shown as the lines are typed -- the scenario above
# is that -- so by the time somebody presses save they have usually
# seen it already. The check at save is for the case where they have
# not: a line typed and left half finished, a figure corrected in one
# place and not the other.
#
# Saying "not matching" and stopping there would leave the user to do
# the arithmetic themselves. So three answers are offered, and what
# separates them is what becomes of the lines that were typed. Edit
# the split goes back to the window with those lines still in it,
# which is the answer for somebody who can now see which of the two
# figures is wrong and wants to fix it. Add the remainder puts the
# difference in a line of its own, which balances the split and
# leaves the amount visible in a category instead of hidden in a
# rounding nobody can find. Cancel the save abandons the edit: the
# window closes, the typed lines go with it, and the split is the one
# it was opened on.
#
# Cancel and edit are both "do not save this", and the difference
# between them is the only thing about this dialog anyone has to
# think about. Edit keeps the work and stays; cancel discards it and
# leaves. Offering one without the other would make somebody choose
# between losing a figure they have just typed and not being able to
# leave the window at all.
#
# The remainder goes to Expenses:Uncategorized, not to Unassigned. The
# two kinds of unknown are set out at the top of this file: Unassigned
# is for money whose other account nobody has worked out, and it is a
# real account whose balance is a work list. A split line is a
# category line, so a remainder nobody has categorised is the other
# unknown -- the one that appears on a spending report as
# Uncategorized and asks to be given a heading.
#
# A split whose lines come to more than the transaction is the same
# question with the sign turned round, and is offered the same three
# answers. The remainder is then negative, and a line of -30.00 under
# Uncategorized is honest about where the arithmetic went.
Examples: SplitSaveCheck
| Total  | Allocated | Remainder | Saved                                           |
| 184.50 | 184.50    | 0.00      | Saved; nothing is said                          |
| 184.50 | 154.50    | 30.00     | Not matching; cancel, edit or add the remainder |
| 184.50 | 214.50    | -30.00    | Not matching; cancel, edit or add the remainder |

Attributes SplitSaveCheck
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Total     | Money  | 0.00    |
| Allocated | Money  | 0.00    |
| Remainder | Money  | 0.00    |
| Saved     | String |         |

$WIP
DataType SplitMismatchChoice
Description What the user answers when a split does not match its total.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value        | Notes                                                       |
| CancelSave   | The window closes, the typed lines go, nothing is saved     |
| EditSplit    | Back to the window with the lines as typed; nothing saved   |
| AddRemainder | The difference becomes a line under Expenses:Uncategorized  |

