| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary          | Income  | false       |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Insurance     | Expense | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee   | Acme Payroll    |
| Deposit | 3550.00         |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category               | Amount   | Memo       |
| Income:Salary          | -5000.00 | Gross pay  |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00  | Withheld   |
| Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00   | Premium    |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee         | Acme Payroll    |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Split           |
| Amount        | 3550.00         |
Then split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination            | Amount   | Memo      |
| Income:Salary          | -5000.00 | Gross pay |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00  | Withheld  |
| Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00   | Premium   |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account                | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes            |
| Assets:Checking        | 3550.00    | 3550.00        | Net pay          |
| Income:Salary          | -5000.00   | 5000.00        | Gross, as earned |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00    | 1200.00        |                  |
| Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00     | 250.00         |                  |

Scenario A split line may transfer to a real account
Description Part of the paycheque going straight to savings is a split line
# naming an account rather than a category. Nothing special is needed:
# a split line does not care which kind of account it names.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary   | Income | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee   | Acme Payroll    |
| Deposit | 2800.00         |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category       | Amount   | Memo            |
| Income:Salary  | -3000.00 | Gross pay       |
| Assets:Savings | 200.00   | Direct to saver |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee         | Acme Payroll    |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Split           |
| Amount        | 2800.00         |
Then split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination    | Amount   | Memo            |
| Income:Salary  | -3000.00 | Gross pay       |
| Assets:Savings | 200.00   | Direct to saver |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A transaction can name both accounts directly
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description SourceAccount, Destination and Amount are how a transaction is
# stored: the account it lives in, the other side, and the signed
# amount between them. A register line can therefore be read from the
# transaction alone, with no second table to join for the ordinary
# case.
Details A split keeps a table of its own, TransactionSplit, for its lines. An
# ordinary transaction -- the common case -- needs no second table at
# all: SourceAccount and Destination are both already on it.
#
# Every reference below -- SourceAccount, Destination, and a split
# line's own Destination -- is an id underneath, the same as every
# account or category reference elsewhere; AccountPath is this
# specification's reading of it, not what is stored. See the identity
# rule in Accounts.

Scenario An ordinary transaction names both accounts directly
Description The register's category column becomes Destination; nothing is
# created that has to sum to anything, because there is only one row.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
When transaction entered : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T1                 |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking    |
| Date          | 2024-01-15         |
| Payee         | Harris Teeter      |
| Destination   | Expenses:Groceries |
| Amount        | -45.00             |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Ref           | T1                 |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking    |
| Destination   | Expenses:Groceries |
| Amount        | -45.00             |
Then no split lines exist for it

Scenario A split names Split as its destination, and its categories live apart
Description The transaction itself says Split and carries the total; the parts
# are rows of their own, found by the transaction's id, rather than
# folded into the transaction the way Postings folds them today.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary          | Income  | false       |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Insurance     | Expense | false       |
When transaction entered : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T1               |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Date          | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee         | Acme Payroll    |
| Destination   | Split           |
| Amount        | 3550.00         |
When split lines entered : TransactionSplit
| Destination            | Amount   | Memo      |
| Income:Salary          | -5000.00 | Gross pay |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00  | Withheld  |
| Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00   | Premium   |
Then the split lines total the transaction's amount

Scenario A split line that is itself a transfer is matched by hand, not automatically
Description Part of a paycheque goes straight to savings. The savings account
# gets its own, independently downloaded entry later; nothing here
# reaches across to find it, because matching a whole account's
# entry is already a careful, user-driven thing, and a split's line
# is a smaller, less certain thing to be matching blind. See the
# matching rule above.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary   | Income  | false       |
When transaction entered : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T1               |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Date          | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee         | Acme Payroll    |
| Destination   | Split           |
| Amount        | 2800.00         |
When split lines entered : TransactionSplit
| Destination    | Amount   | Memo            |
| Income:Salary  | -3000.00 | Gross pay       |
| Assets:Savings | 200.00   | Direct to saver |
When another entry is downloaded : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T2             |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Savings |
| Date          | 2024-04-16     |
| Destination   | none           |
| Amount        | 200.00         |
Then entries in that account are : TransactionCount Vertical
| Account | Assets:Savings |
| Count   | 1              |
# Just the one, the download: a split line naming a real account is a line of
# Assets:Checking's split, not a row of its own in Assets:Savings' register.
# Nothing here joined them, which is the point.

Attributes TransactionCount
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Account   | AccountPath |         |
| Count     | Integer     | 0       |

Scenario Assigning the other account updates Destination
Description The last resort, and the one that always works, done the new way:
# there is one row for this entry, and assigning the other account is
# changing what its Destination says, not creating or editing a
# second transaction to Unassigned.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       |
| Unassigned             | OtherAsset | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee     | Destination | Amount   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Unassigned  | -1200.00 |
When the other account is assigned : AccountAssignment Vertical
| Ref     | T1                     |
| Account | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Ref         | T1                     |
| Destination | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account    | RawBalance |
| Unassigned | 0.00       |
# Nothing was added and nothing was deleted: the transaction that said
# Unassigned now says which account, which is the whole of the change. The
# suspense balance goes to zero because that transaction no longer names it.

Scenario Matching an entry in another register updates both transactions' Destination
Description The card was imported weeks ago and the payment never paired. Both
# sides already exist as their own row, each with a real account as
# SourceAccount and Unassigned as Destination. Matching them points
# each one at the other's account; neither row is deleted and nothing
# is merged into one.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       |
| Unassigned             | OtherAsset | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount          | Date       | Payee            | Destination | Amount   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking        | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Unassigned  | -1200.00 |
| T2  | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Unassigned  | 1200.00  |
When the other entry is matched : EntryMatch Vertical
| Ref      | T1 |
| OtherRef | T2 |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Ref | SourceAccount          | Destination            |
| T1  | Assets:Checking        | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
| T2  | Liabilities:Chase Visa | Assets:Checking         |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account    | RawBalance |
| Unassigned | 0.00       |
# Each keeps its own date and its own payee -- the bank's word for it on one
# side, the card's on the other. The fifteen-day gap was the reason nothing
# paired them automatically and is no reason not to point them at each other
# now. Neither row names Unassigned any more, so the suspense balance is zero.

Entity TransactionSplit
Description One category line of a split, kept apart from the transaction that
# carries the total and the account it was entered or downloaded
# against. Only a split has rows here: an ordinary transaction's
# destination is a plain account or category, named directly on the
# transaction, and needs none of this.
| Attribute     | Type          | Default | Notes                            |
| TransactionId | TransactionId | none    | Which split this line belongs to; a unique id, not a name |
| Destination   | AccountPath   |         | A category, or an account for a line entered by hand as a transfer |
| Amount        | Money         | 0.00    | Positive is money leaving SourceAccount |
| Memo          | Text          | none    |                                   |

Collection TransactionSplits
Description The lines of every split in the book.
| DataType         | Minimum | Maximum | Notes |
| TransactionSplit | 0       | 50      |       |

BusinessRule A split's lines are what is totalled against its transaction's amount
Description The transaction itself is never out of balance under this shape --
# it names one amount against one source account, and nothing sums
# against it. Only a split can be short or over, because only a
# split has more than one figure that is supposed to agree with
# another. See the existing shortfall scenario above, which already
# checks exactly this.
Examples: SplitTotalCheck
| TransactionAmount | LineTotal | Short | Notes                    |
| 184.50             | 184.50    | 0.00  | Agrees                   |
| 184.50             | 154.50    | 30.00 | Short by thirty          |
| 3550.00            | 3550.00   | 0.00  | A paycheque split agrees |

Attributes SplitTotalCheck
| Attribute         | Type   | Default |
| TransactionAmount | Money  | 0.00    |
| LineTotal         | Money  | 0.00    |
| Short             | Money  | 0.00    |
| Notes             | String | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# When the other side is not known
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario A transaction with no category posts to Uncategorized
Description An import knows one side of a transaction and often not the other.
# Rather than refuse it, or leave the book out of balance, the other
# side goes to an Uncategorized account chosen by the sign, so that
# reports stay readable and the work left to do is visible in one
# place. See ImportOfx.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-05-01      |
| Payee   | Unknown Shop    |
| Payment | 23.75           |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-05-01             |
| Payee         | Unknown Shop           |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking        |
| Destination   | Expenses:Uncategorized |
| Amount        | -23.75                 |

Scenario An uncategorised deposit posts to income
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-05-02      |
| Payee   | Unknown Source  |
| Deposit | 500.00          |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-05-02           |
| Payee         | Unknown Source       |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking      |
| Destination   | Income:Uncategorized |
| Amount        | 500.00               |

BusinessRule Which Uncategorized account the other side takes
Description Chosen by the sign of the transaction's own amount, so an expense
# never shows as negative income.
Examples: UncategorizedChoice
| KnownAmount | OtherAccount           | Notes                    |
| -23.75      | Expenses:Uncategorized | Money left the account   |
| 500.00      | Income:Uncategorized   | Money arrived            |
| 0.00        | Expenses:Uncategorized | A zero amount; by convention |

Attributes UncategorizedChoice
| Attribute    | Type        | Default |
| KnownAmount  | Money       | 0.00    |
| OtherAccount | AccountPath |         |
| Notes        | String      | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Changing and removing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Changing the amount of an ordinary transaction moves both sides
Description An ordinary transaction carries one amount and its destination takes
# the opposite by construction, so editing the amount in the register
# moves both sides at once without asking anything.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 |
When amount changed : AmountChange Vertical
| Ref     | T1    |
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Payment | 52.10 |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Ref           | T1                 |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking    |
| Destination   | Expenses:Groceries |
| Amount        | -52.10             |

Attributes AmountChange
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| Account   | AccountPath    |         |
| Payment   | Money          | 0.00    |
| Deposit   | Money          | 0.00    |

Scenario Changing the amount of a split does not guess which line to adjust
Description A split has more than one line, so there is no one other side to
# move, and the shortfall is reported and the split window is opened
# instead.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-04-02 | Split       | -154.50 |
Given split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination               | Amount |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00 |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50  |
When amount changed : AmountChange Vertical
| Ref     | T1              |
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Payment | 200.00          |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                  |
| T1 is a split; the split is 45.50 short of 200.00       |

Scenario Changing the category changes the destination
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Dining    | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination        | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 |
When category changed : CategoryChange Vertical
| Ref         | T1                 |
| FromAccount | Expenses:Groceries |
| ToAccount   | Expenses:Dining    |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Ref           | T1              |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Expenses:Dining |
| Amount        | -45.00          |

Attributes CategoryChange
| Attribute   | Type           | Default |
| Ref         | TransactionRef |         |
| FromAccount | AccountPath    |         |
| ToAccount   | AccountPath    |         |

Scenario Deleting a transaction removes it from both registers at once
Description A transaction is one record naming both sides, so a delete is always
# of the whole of it, from whichever register it is asked for. There
# is no half of it that could be left behind.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination        | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-16 | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 |
When transaction deleted : TransactionSelect
| Ref |
| T1  |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination        | Amount |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-16 | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 |

Scenario Deleting a split takes its lines with it
Description The lines of a split belong to the transaction that carries the
# total and have nothing to belong to once it is gone. Left behind
# they would be the split of a transaction that does not exist: the
# categories would still carry the money on every report, and
# nothing in the register would say where it came from.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Expense  | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | Expense  | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries        | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot    | Split              | -154.50 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-04-03 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00  |
Given split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination               | Amount | Memo    |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00 | Paint   |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50  | Compost |
When transaction deleted : TransactionSelect
| Ref |
| T1  |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination        | Amount |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-04-03 | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 |
Then split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination | Amount | Memo |
# No rows left. T2 is not a split and never had any, so what this says is that
# T1's two lines went with T1.

Scenario A reconciled transaction warns before it is changed
Description A finished reconciliation agreed with a statement. Changing a
# transaction inside it breaks that agreement, so it is allowed but
# not quietly.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination        | Amount | Cleared    |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 | Reconciled |
When amount changed : AmountChange Vertical
| Ref     | T1              |
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Payment | 52.10           |
Then warned that : Warning
| Message                                                             |
| This posting is reconciled; changing it will alter a reconciled balance |

Attributes Warning
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Message   | Text |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Clearing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario The two sides of a downloaded transfer clear separately
Description Money leaves checking on one statement and arrives in savings on
# another, often days apart. Each account downloaded its own entry,
# so each side is a transaction in its own right carrying its own
# Cleared, and one can be cleared while the other is not.
Details This is why the status is on the transaction rather than anywhere
# else: the transaction is one account's own entry, and clearing is
# one account's bank reporting one row of its own statement.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Checking | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination     | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-03-01 | Assets:Savings  | -500.00 | Uncleared |
| T2  | Assets:Savings  | 2024-03-03 | Assets:Checking | 500.00  | Uncleared |
When transaction cleared : TransactionSelect
| Ref |
| T1  |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | Cleared   |
| T2  | Assets:Savings  | Uncleared |
# A transfer entered by hand is two transactions as well -- see the transfer
# scenario above -- so this is the same shape whether the two sides were
# downloaded separately or typed once, and each clears on its own statement.
